WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.734
-You feel the love there?
-Dude, I am so underdressed.

00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:05.200
You didn't tell me
what I was following.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.334
Are you kidding me?

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:08.267
-Sorry. There's John Oliver
feathers around.

00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:09.701
I apologize if you're allergic.
-No apology.

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:10.834
I actually ran into him earlier.

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:12.300
He tried to sell me a CD
at Times Square

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.434
in that same outfit.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:15.067
-Yeah, that wasn't him.
That wasn't --

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:16.834
-That wasn't?
-That was Elmo, yeah.

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:18.234
-Oh, got it.

00:00:18.234 --> 00:00:21.200
-But I want to talk to you about
everything in your whole career,

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.934
but I want to get to know
you a little bit, too.

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:26.000
I just want to say,
your ProbleMATTic World Tour --

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.133
you announced it.

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:30.767
And in 48 hours,
you sold 600,000 tickets.

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:32.000
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.567
-Dude.
-Insane. I know. I know.

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:35.167
-What does that feel like?

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:36.868
-I mean,
it feels incredible, man.

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:38.968
I went from -- I mean,
literally, July of last year,

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:40.634
I was considering
quitting comedy and everything.

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:42.334
I couldn't sell any tickets
at any comedy club.

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:44.300
I wasn't getting
any kind of break.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.000
So, from going from
playing clubs for 35 people

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.200
who got free tickets to we broke
Ticketmaster, which is insane.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.400
So, yeah, Travis Kelce,
if it doesn't work out,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.467
holler at your boy.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:58.467
-Yeah. Yeah.
-It's been unreal, man.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:00.767
My entire life has changed
in a matter of like 14 months.

00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:02.000
I'm so grateful.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.801
-I heard about this story.

00:01:04.801 --> 00:01:08.467
I kind of related to you because
you started comedy around 15?

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:10.033
-Yeah, 15. Yeah.
-Wow!

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:11.901
-My my grandpa used to take me

00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:14.267
to the Columbus Funny Bone
in Ohio every Wednesday,

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:16.100
and it was a bringer show,
like a lot of open mics are,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.200
where you have to bring --

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.567
-Oh, a bringer, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:21.000
Usually, like five people
who have to pay for tickets.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.801
-The way it works is,
if you go and perform,

00:01:22.801 --> 00:01:24.133
you have to actually
bring five people

00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:25.801
that buy tickets and buy drinks.

00:01:25.801 --> 00:01:27.734
-Yeah. And hope they laugh.
-And hope that they laugh.

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:29.467
Yeah, they just want business.
-Oh, of course.

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:30.634
-They don't really care about
your career.

00:01:30.634 --> 00:01:32.400
-No, not at all.
And all my friends were still

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.501
in high school, obviously,
so they couldn't come attend.

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:35.968
So my grandpa would
buy five tickets

00:01:35.968 --> 00:01:39.601
so the club would think I had
five people there to perform.

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:42.834
And then he would just watch me
tank week after week after week.

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:44.267
-He was the only guy?

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:46.067
-No, but he loved it, though.
He was my biggest supporter.

00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:47.234
Like, if it wasn't
for my grandpa,

00:01:47.234 --> 00:01:49.234
I'd have absolutely
no sense of humor

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:50.701
and I never would have got
my start into comedy.

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:52.167
So --
-Got to give it up for grandpa.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:54.033
-Oh, thank you.
-Good to have that support.

00:01:54.033 --> 00:01:55.367
-Thank you, thank you.

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.567
-What -- Do you remember
what your set was

00:01:57.567 --> 00:01:59.334
when you were 15 years old?

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:02.133
-Oh, nonsense.
Not stand-up at all.

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.868
I also, for the first six months
of doing stand-up,

00:02:03.868 --> 00:02:04.968
had no idea what a set was.

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:06.100
Nobody told me.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.367
I would just go up there
and do five minutes

00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:09.701
of, like, ridiculous things
over and over again.

00:02:09.701 --> 00:02:11.100
But it was never like kid stuff.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.334
I never was -- I was never
telling, like, school jokes.

00:02:13.334 --> 00:02:15.534
I was never making,
like, poop jokes or anything.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:17.100
-No, it wasn't --
-It was always for adults,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.801
because I was a kid performing
for adults in a comedy club,

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:21.601
which I think inevitably
helped me transition,

00:02:21.601 --> 00:02:24.767
because around the time
I was like 23, 24,

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:26.334
people start looking at you
a little bit differently.

00:02:26.334 --> 00:02:27.934
You're no longer,
like, the novelty of like,

00:02:27.934 --> 00:02:29.567
"Oh, he's a kid,
give him a chance."

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:31.567
At 24, people were like,
"We paid to get in here.

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:33.434
Make us laugh."
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:34.834
-So I think that helped
that transition

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:36.100
be a little bit smoother,

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.934
that I never really played
into the young aspect.

00:02:38.934 --> 00:02:42.200
-And you were saying earlier --
I'm sorry that I went over it --

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.400
you were thinking
about quitting?

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.534
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:44.534 --> 00:02:45.968
Dude, I mean,
I had been doing comedy

00:02:45.968 --> 00:02:50.234
for 11 years as of last year,
10 of it in L.A.

00:02:50.234 --> 00:02:52.567
And it's just --
It beats you down hearing,

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:53.801
like, so much rejection.

00:02:53.801 --> 00:02:55.000
Like, I couldn't get
a special anywhere.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.534
I couldn't get on
a TV show anywhere.

00:02:57.534 --> 00:02:59.367
I couldn't get on
"The Jimmy Fallon Show."

00:02:59.367 --> 00:03:01.501
-Oh, my God. Wow!
-I know.

00:03:01.501 --> 00:03:03.033
I submitted a couple times.
We'll talk after.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.234
-Okay, yeah.

00:03:04.234 --> 00:03:05.834
-No, all of these things
just weren't adding up,

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:08.801
and after a certain point,
you go, "Am I delusional?

00:03:08.801 --> 00:03:10.033
Like, maybe I'm not funny.

00:03:10.033 --> 00:03:11.467
Maybe I'm not
supposed to do this."

00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:14.267
And then just one random video
kind of changed everything.

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:15.968
-Yeah, it was a TikTok video.

00:03:15.968 --> 00:03:18.467
-Yeah. I reluctantly started
posting on TikTok

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:19.667
beginning of last year.

00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:21.000
I've always hated social media.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.434
I think it's so negative,
and it just stresses me out.

00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:24.801
But one day, I was just like,

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:26.601
"How long can I go against
the grain and it not work?"

00:03:26.601 --> 00:03:28.434
So I started
posting sporadically.

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:30.200
And then I was at the
Just For Laughs festival,

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.601
which is, like,
the most notorious

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:33.467
comedy festival in comedy,

00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:35.000
besides the
New York Comedy Festival --

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.200
-Where I got my start.
-Is it really?

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.033
-Yeah. New Faces.
-No way. Never got New Faces.

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:41.734
-Alright, well --
-I know, I know, I know.

00:03:41.734 --> 00:03:44.234
So, I'm at this festival,

00:03:44.234 --> 00:03:45.601
and even that was
a funny circumstance,

00:03:45.601 --> 00:03:47.701
because over the pandemic,
my friend Paul Elia,

00:03:47.701 --> 00:03:49.534
who is one of my best friends,
great comic --

00:03:49.534 --> 00:03:51.868
we developed this show over the
pandemic that was a dual-hosted,

00:03:51.868 --> 00:03:53.000
improvised show.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.200
-Yeah.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.434
-And Just For Laughs wanted to
pick up that show

00:03:56.434 --> 00:03:59.133
for the festival,
but they didn't want me.

00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:00.534
They wanted Paul and not me.

00:04:00.534 --> 00:04:02.100
And Paul, being a good friend,
was like,

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.133
"I refuse to do the show
without him.

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:04.400
Absolutely not."

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.501
So they said, "Well,
we're not going to pay him.

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:06.834
We're not going to pay
for his travel.

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:08.300
But if he wants to perform,
he can perform."

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.234
So I was like, "You know what?
I'll swallow my pride.

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:12.400
I want to experience
what this festival is about."

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.601
We went. It was arguably
the best show of the festival.

00:04:15.601 --> 00:04:16.934
But before that show,
the same night,

00:04:16.934 --> 00:04:18.934
we were at dinner, and I was
about to post this clip

00:04:18.934 --> 00:04:22.200
called "The Lazy Hero,"
which I had already edited.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.968
It was at a show
in Phoenix, Arizona,

00:04:23.968 --> 00:04:26.868
for like 75 people,
and I was just sitting there.

00:04:26.868 --> 00:04:29.601
I was so depleted and depressed
at, you know, being at a place

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:31.801
I wasn't even invited
to perform at.

00:04:31.801 --> 00:04:33.133
And I was like,
"This is so stupid.

00:04:33.133 --> 00:04:34.901
Why am I even
bothering posting this

00:04:34.901 --> 00:04:36.167
if it's not going
to do anything?"

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:37.868
And my friend was like,
"You already edited it.

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:40.467
Just post it, and then
we'll have a fun time tonight."

00:04:40.467 --> 00:04:42.100
And then that video,
over the next couple of days,

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:45.701
went insanely viral,
like 20 to 30 million views.

00:04:45.701 --> 00:04:48.868
And then that made every other
video on my profile go viral.

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:51.334
And then everything just kind of
exploded out of nowhere.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:55.701
-30 million views.
-Yeah, dude. Insane.

00:04:55.701 --> 00:04:57.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
Insane. Life-changing, man.

00:04:57.434 --> 00:04:58.701
I'm so grateful.

00:04:58.701 --> 00:05:00.400
-Isn't that wild,
how that happened?

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.200
-The irony of something
you dreaded doing

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.501
changing your entire life.

00:05:04.501 --> 00:05:07.133
So take more chances.
-Yeah, that's a good thing.

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:08.634
Yeah, take more chances.
Why not?

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:10.033
Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:12.300
-But I also love
how people go like,

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.267
"Oh, Matt Rife --
yeah, overnight success."

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:17.868
-12-year overnight success, man.
It takes forever.

00:05:17.868 --> 00:05:19.133
But you have to look back
at those moments,

00:05:19.133 --> 00:05:20.901
and all the times
you thought you were ready

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:23.501
for a certain opportunity,
you probably weren't.

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:25.667
Now I'm in a position right now
where there's a lot of pressure

00:05:25.667 --> 00:05:27.501
to stand behind what I've built
on social media

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:29.901
in my live performances,
and had I not had

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:32.133
these years
of training leading up to it,

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:34.000
these shows might not be
as good as they are now.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.767
Now I feel like
I'm in the proper position

00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:36.901
at the proper time.

00:05:36.901 --> 00:05:38.300
-Good for you.
-Thank you.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.901
-I'm happy for you.
-Thank you.

00:05:39.901 --> 00:05:42.000
-I'm a fan. You're very funny.
-Thank you so much.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.300
-You have a new special
November 15th on Netflix.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.434
-Yeah, yeah.
"Natural Selection."

00:05:46.434 --> 00:05:47.601
-"Natural Selection."

00:05:47.601 --> 00:05:48.701
-You were supposed
to say it. I'm sorry.

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:50.000
-No. No. We both should say it.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.634
"Natural Selection."
-"Natural Selection."

00:05:51.634 --> 00:05:53.934
You guys say it?
-"Natural Selection"!

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:55.901
-Now everyone's saying it.
Alright, this is fantastic.

00:05:55.901 --> 00:05:58.167
-You're so fired.
-I know, yeah.

00:05:58.167 --> 00:06:00.400
You shot it in D.C.
-Yeah.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.667
-Why D.C.?

00:06:01.667 --> 00:06:03.534
-D.C. has always been
my favorite city

00:06:03.534 --> 00:06:04.567
in the entire country.

00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:06.100
I love it there.
It's a beautiful city.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:07.234
I'm obsessed with history.

00:06:07.234 --> 00:06:09.634
I've been to all the museums
dozens of times.

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:11.234
-It's kind of fascinating.
-The parks there.

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:12.534
Yeah, I'm obsessed with it.

00:06:12.534 --> 00:06:14.167
-What was the venue?

00:06:14.167 --> 00:06:15.834
-It's the Daughters of
the American Revolution

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:17.501
at Constitu-- Cons--
Constitution Hall,

00:06:17.501 --> 00:06:19.434
if my mouth decides to work.

00:06:19.434 --> 00:06:23.367
And I got an opportunity to open
for Dane Cook there in 2019,

00:06:23.367 --> 00:06:24.868
and I fell in love with it.

00:06:24.868 --> 00:06:26.734
And I had already done a little
bit of research on the venue.

00:06:26.734 --> 00:06:28.534
It's where Eddie Murphy
filmed "Delirious,"

00:06:28.534 --> 00:06:30.567
which is arguably one of
the greatest comedy specials

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:31.667
of all time.

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:32.934
Robin Williams
did a special there.

00:06:32.934 --> 00:06:34.400
Katt Williams
did a special there.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:35.834
Presidents have spoken there.

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:38.200
And it just has this amazing,
like, Colosseum-esque theme

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.567
to it that I just really thought
captured what we're trying

00:06:40.567 --> 00:06:42.834
to get our point across on,
on this special.

00:06:42.834 --> 00:06:45.133
So it was inevitably
the best possible location,

00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:46.434
and I couldn't be
more proud of it.

00:06:46.434 --> 00:06:47.968
-Oh, yeah, it looks gorgeous,
and it's very funny.

00:06:47.968 --> 00:06:49.200
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.434
Here's Matt Rife
in "Natural Selection."

00:06:51.434 --> 00:06:52.601
Take a look at this.

00:06:52.601 --> 00:06:53.968
-Get this through your head.

00:06:53.968 --> 00:06:57.300
Astrology is not
this magical life guideline

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.334
that predetermines
your future in the stars.

00:07:00.334 --> 00:07:01.701
No, none of that.

00:07:01.701 --> 00:07:02.901
Your future is dependent

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:04.734
by your own thoughts,
opinions and actions.

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:07.300
You are in complete control
of how your future turns out.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.934
It's not up here. It's in here.
The whole time, it's up to you.

00:07:11.934 --> 00:07:14.400
It has nothing to do
with the stars, man.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.167
Just because Jupiter has a ring
and you don't doesn't mean...

00:07:21.067 --> 00:07:23.067
doesn't mean that's who you're
supposed to look up to

00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:25.567
for all this
magical advice, man.

00:07:25.567 --> 00:07:27.434
-Come on. That's what I'm
talking about, buddy.

00:07:27.434 --> 00:07:29.000
Matt Rife!

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.467
"Natural Selection" premieres
November 15th on Netflix.

00:07:32.467 --> 00:07:33.734
Congratulations.
-Thank you so much.

00:07:33.734 --> 00:07:35.501
-Come back, please.
-Really, thank you so much.

00:07:35.501 --> 00:07:37.033
We'll be right back
with more "Tonight Show."

00:07:37.033 --> 00:07:38.968
Stick around, everybody.
Matt Rife.

